`src/str.js:7`:
```js
function truncate(s, n) {
  if (s.length <= n) return s;
  return s.slice(0, n) + '…';
}
```

Two caveats on the implementation, both deliberate:

- **No input validation.** `trim` doesn't validate either, so I matched it. `truncate(null, 3)` throws, and a negative `n` yields just `'…'` since `slice` clamps.
- **`length` counts UTF-16 code units, not characters.** Astral characters (emoji, some CJK) can be split mid-surrogate-pair, producing a lone surrogate before the ellipsis. Fixing it properly means `[...s]` and counting code points — still not grapheme-correct for combining marks or ZWJ emoji sequences. I left it as the simple version to match the rest of the file; tell me if the inputs are user-facing text and I'll make it code-point-safe.

